#  webkit-wpe-device-orientation.patch: make DeviceOrientation/DeviceMotion
#  reachable on a non-Cocoa port. ENABLE_DEVICE_ORIENTATION is defined PRIVATE
#  OFF and enabled by no port; even switched on, the controllers are only ever
#  created under PLATFORM(IOS_FAMILY) (through Document), so on WPE
#  LocalDOMWindow::deviceOrientationController() resolves a Page supplement that
#  nothing provides and no sensor event can fire. Adds the two provide*To()
#  functions every other supplement already has, plus the WebProcess-side
#  clients, and installs them from WebPage.
#
# ---
DeviceOrientation and DeviceMotion for WPE.

Everything behind ENABLE(DEVICE_ORIENTATION) in WebCore is generic and present in
the tarball: both controllers, the client interfaces, DeviceOrientationData /
DeviceMotionData, both events and the permission controller. What is missing is
anything that connects them to a port.

On PLATFORM(IOS_FAMILY), Document owns a DeviceOrientationClientIOS and a
DeviceMotionClientIOS and constructs the controllers directly
(Document.cpp:691-696). Every other port goes through
DeviceOrientationController::from(Page*) — a Supplement<Page> lookup
(LocalDOMWindow.cpp:2130) — and nothing in the tree ever provides that
supplement, so it is null forever. Enabling the build flag alone therefore adds
window.DeviceOrientationEvent while guaranteeing no event behind it.

Not reusable, for the record: WebDeviceOrientationUpdateProvider in
WebProcess/WebCoreSupport already implements exactly the IPC receiver this needs
and contains no Cocoa code, but every header it includes
(WebCore/DeviceOrientationUpdateProvider.h, MotionManagerClient.h) is one of the
PLATFORM(IOS_FAMILY) sources Apple strips from WPE releases, as are the two iOS
clients. There is nothing to revive.

So: provideDeviceOrientationTo() / provideDeviceMotionTo(), modelled on
provideGeolocationTo() (GeolocationController.cpp:195), and two small clients
that hold the last sample and track whether WebCore wants updates.

Client ownership differs from geolocation's on purpose. GeolocationClient is
RefCounted and GeolocationController keeps a RefPtr; DeviceOrientationClient is
not refcounted and the controller keeps a WeakRef, with
deviceOrientationControllerDestroyed() as the teardown signal. The obvious
`delete this` in that callback is wrong here — WTF_OVERRIDE_DELETE_FOR_CHECKED_PTR
gives the base class a destroying operator delete that asserts on the exact
object size, so destroying a derived client through it is a size mismatch.
WebPage owns both clients instead. That is safe in either order: the Page is
torn down in WebPage::close(), and WebPage::~WebPage asserts !m_page, so the
controllers are always destroyed before the clients are.

The clients are passive by design. Sensors live in the UI process (QtSensors ->
sensorfw) and samples are pushed down to orientationChanged()/motionChanged();
the WebProcess never touches the sensor stack, matching how geolocation already
avoids it. startUpdating()/stopUpdating() currently only record the state — the
message that tells the UI process it can stop sampling is the next step, and
until it lands the embedder has to decide for itself when to run the sensors.

See docs/investigations/device-orientation.md.
